+- **A direct wallet sign-in** (`wallet` block: `{"audience": "0x<this jot's
+  id>", "domains": ["notes.example.org"], "accept": ["0x<eyesoff-ai id>"]}`).
+  The visitor's own wallet signs an EIP-4361 message for this host and
+  deployment, and jot verifies it; no enclave.host account and no issuer are
+  involved (eyesoff-ai's `PLATFORM-sso.md` has the format). It names
+  `wallet_0x<address>`, a notebook of its own: the same wallet signed in
+  through `sso` is a different notebook, never silently the same one.
+  eyesoff-ai's `$user` carries that name through `X-User` too.
+  - **Contract wallets.** With `contract_wallets` (pinned JSON-RPC readers on
+    distinct hosts) in the block, smart-contract wallets sign in too, by the
+    agreeing-snapshot quorum: deployed ones through EIP-1271, and ones not
+    deployed yet through EIP-6492 (each reader simulates the wallet's factory
+    call in one deployless call). One lying reader can neither approve nor
+    veto.
+  - **Signers outside the browser.** The UI's *Another signer…* covers a
+    passkey account, a Safe, a hardware or command-line wallet: the page shows
+    the exact message and takes back the signature, which jot verifies like
+    any other.
+
+**Keeping an existing notebook with a wallet.** With both blocks set, a
+signed-in person can link a wallet to their account (the UI's *Link a wallet*,
+or `POST /api/link/challenge` then `POST /api/link`).
+
+What it takes:
+- an Enclave sign-in for this notebook from the last ten minutes;
+- a wallet signature naming that account, that sign-in and this notebook.
+
+What is written:
+- two create-only records under `<prefix>.jot/links/`.
+
+What follows:
+- That wallet then opens the account's existing notebook. Nothing is moved or
+  re-sealed.
+
+What is refused:
+- relinking either side;
+- a wallet that already has notes of its own (linking would hide them);
+- an old session token;
+- a routine wallet sign-in.
+
+How links are stored:
+- **Authenticated, not trusted.** Each record carries this app's HMAC under the
+  link key: `link_key` (a `$SECRET`), or one derived from `master_key` when no
+  `link_key` is configured.
+- **What a record is bound to:** this deployment, the bucket, the prefix, its
+  direction and both identities.
+- **What links nothing:** forged or copied records, and every record when no key
+  is configured.
+- **Store requirements:** the bucket must honour `If-None-Match: *` and
+  `If-Match` on PUT, as AWS S3, R2 and minio do.
+- **Failure behaviour:** nothing is ever deleted. A half-made link is inert and
+  is completed or replaced, by compare-and-swap, on the next proven attempt.
+- **Unlinking:** there is none. The operator can delete the two objects.
+
+**When to link.** A link needs a fresh Enclave sign-in for the account, and jot
+issues no account credential of its own. An existing account must therefore
+link once while the Enclave issuer still signs it in. After that, the stored
+link serves with no issuer at all, including for a contract wallet not yet
+deployed. An account that never links before the issuer stops cannot reach its
+notes again.
+
+eyesoff-ai's `PLATFORM-sso.md` has the full contract.
